UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 7: Aural skills and listening', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Structure is the order of sections, and this lesson uses two labels only. Section A is one musical idea. Section B is a different idea you can hear. If the first idea comes back unchanged, it is still A, because same material keeps the same letter. Different can mean the words, the rhythm, or both. A new line on the page is not automatically B. You will use a short original chant, spoken or softly sung, then label what you heard. Structure lets a listener predict a return or notice a real change.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nPerform \"Boots in the hall, boots in the hall\" and then \"Quiet now, coats down\". Which line is section A, which is section B, and what is one difference you can hear?\n\nFEEDBACK\nA strong answer labels the boots line as A because it states one idea and repeats it, and labels the coats line as B because the words and rhythm are new. One real difference is enough: the second line does not echo \"boots in the hall\", and its rhythm is a short call plus two words rather than a doubled phrase. Saying \"the second line is B because it is second\" would also label an exact repeat as B, which is wrong. Quote a word from each section as evidence.\n\nWATCH FOR\nAny second line is section B, even when it copies the first line exactly in words and rhythm. That fails because an exact copy is more of section A, not a contrast. B is reserved for material that sounds different. If you cannot name a heard change, do not spend the letter B.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCreate and label this new chant, not the rain chant and not the boots chant: A is \"Kettle click, kettle click\" and B is \"Steam up, lid down, wait\". Write the form as A then B and state one difference in words or rhythm. Perform it aloud once, then mark the repeated half inside A. Your evidence should mention click or steam so a reader knows which section you mean.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Music","learning_intention":"Structure is the order of sections, and this lesson uses two labels only. Section A is one musical idea. Section B is a different idea you can hear. If the first idea comes back unchanged, it is still A, because same material keeps the same letter. Different can mean the words, the rhythm, or both. A new line on the page is not automatically B. You will use a short original chant, spoken or softly sung, then label what you heard. Structure lets a listener predict a return or notice a real change.","lesson_number":7,"lesson_role":"Learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MU4-LIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Create and label this new chant, not the rain chant and not the boots chant: A is \"Kettle click, kettle click\" and B is \"Steam up, lid down, wait\". Write the form as A then B and state one difference in words or rhythm.  Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using structure on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Any second line is section B, even when it copies the first line exactly in words and rhythm.. Correction: A strong answer labels the boots line as A because it states one idea and repeats it, and labels the coats line as B. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Any second line is section B, even when it copies the first line exactly in words and rhythm. That fails because an exact copy is more of section A, not a contrast. B is reserved for material that sounds different. If you cannot name a heard change, do not spend the letter B. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating structure on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with structure, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about structure with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for structure. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for structure. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A strong answer labels the boots line as A because it states one idea and repeats it, and labels the coats line as B","Any second line is section B, even when it copies the first line exactly in words and rhythm","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work"],"question":"Perform \"Boots in the hall, boots in the hall\" and then \"Quiet now, coats down\". Which line is section A, which is section B, and what is one difference you can hear?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for structure; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of section with clear evidence","Any second line is section B, even when it copies the first line exactly in words and rhythm","Structure is the order of sections, and this lesson uses two labels only","A strong answer labels the boots line as A because it states one idea and repeats it, and labels the coats line as B"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with structure?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named section, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply structure to new material and name the section used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Any second line is section B, even when it copies the first line exactly in words and rhythm","Name section only, without linking it to structure"],"question":"Which response best applies structure with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on structure. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Any second line is section B, even when it copies the first line exactly in words and rhythm","A strong answer labels the boots line as A because it states one idea and repeats it, and labels the coats line as B","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named"],"question":"Which response best evaluates elements, techniques and audience effect when analysing structure?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for structure","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Any second line is section B, even when it copies the first line exactly in words and rhythm"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about structure?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Structure is the order of sections, and this lesson uses two labels) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Chant A as \"Rain on the roof, rain on the roof\", using the same rhythm both times","Any second line is section B, even when it copies the first line exactly in words and rhythm","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about structure?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful"],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for structure?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Any second line is section B, even when it copies the first line exactly in words and rhythm","Name section only, with no linked example","Create and label this new chant, not the rain chant and not the boots chant: A is \"Kettle click, kettle click\" and B is","Retype the worked example with no new example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies structure to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about structure with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating structure on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using structure on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for structure. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with structure, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Any second line is section B, even when it copies the first line exactly in words and rhythm.. Correction: A strong answer labels the boots line as A because it states one idea and repeats it, and labels the coats line as B. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-music-y9-w04-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 4 alignment to Music 7–10 (2024) outcome MU4-LIS-01. Content-point mapping is pending. Not NESA verified. No copyrighted recordings are required; examples are original descriptions or student-made sounds.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise structure.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Music","success_criteria":["I can use structure with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify section and explain why it matters.","I can practise repeat independently on a new prompt.","I can notice contrast needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Structure is the order of sections, and this lesson uses two labels only.","options":["Structure is the order of sections, and this lesson uses two labels only","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about structure?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nChant A as \"Rain on the roof, rain on the roof\", using the same rhythm both times. Chant B as \"Thunder roll and then stop\", with a longer word on \"roll\" and a stop at the end. The form is A then B. B is not a repeat, because the words and the rhythm both change. Clap the rhythm of each line. The roof line splits into two matching halves. The thunder line does not. Write A and B in boxes and jot the one difference you clapped.\n\nTRY FIRST\nPerform \"Boots in the hall, boots in the hall\" and then \"Quiet now, coats down\". Which line is section A, which is section B, and what is one difference you can hear?\n\nFEEDBACK\nA strong answer labels the boots line as A because it states one idea and repeats it, and labels the coats line as B because the words and rhythm are new. One real difference is enough: the second line does not echo \"boots in the hall\", and its rhythm is a short call plus two words rather than a doubled phrase. Saying \"the second line is B because it is second\" would also label an exact repeat as B, which is wrong. Quote a word from each section as evidence.","explain":"Structure is the order of sections, and this lesson uses two labels only. Section A is one musical idea. Section B is a different idea you can hear. If the first idea comes back unchanged, it is still A, because same material keeps the same letter. Different can mean the words, the rhythm, or both. A new line on the page is not automatically B. You will use a short original chant, spoken or softly sung, then label what you heard. Structure lets a listener predict a return or notice a real change.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Any second line is section B, even when it copies the first line exactly in words and rhythm. That fails because an exact copy is more of section A, not a contrast. B is reserved for material that sounds different. If you cannot name a heard change, do not spend the letter B. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Two boxes contrast a repeated section A with a different section B.","caption":"B is a heard contrast, not merely the second line.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Section A</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Same chant heard again</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Section B</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">A chant that sounds different</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Chant A as \"Rain on the roof, rain on the roof\", using the same rhythm both times. Chant B as \"Thunder roll and then sto Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Chant A as \"Rain on the roof, rain on the roof\", using the same rhythm both times. Chant B as \"Thunder roll and then sto Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about structure?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nChant A as \"Rain on the roof, rain on the roof\", using the same rhythm both times. Chant B as \"Thunder roll and then stop\", with a longer word on \"roll\" and a stop at the end. The form is A then B. B is not a repeat, because the words and the rhythm both change. Clap the rhythm of each line. The roof line splits into two matching halves. The thunder line does not. Write A and B in boxes and jot the one difference you clapped. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Two boxes contrast a same section with a different section.","caption":"An exact repeat keeps the letter A.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Same</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Words and rhythm return</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Different</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Words or rhythm change</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about structure?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAny second line is section B, even when it copies the first line exactly in words and rhythm. That fails because an exact copy is more of section A, not a contrast. B is reserved for material that sounds different. If you cannot name a heard change, do not spend the letter B. Here is a more helpful example: Chant A as \"Rain on the roof, rain on the roof\", using the same rhythm both times. Chant B as \"Thunder roll and then stop\", with a longer word on \"roll\" and a stop at the end. The form is A then B. B is not a repeat, because the words and the rhythm both change. Clap the rhythm of each line. The roof line splits into two matching halves. The thunder line does not. Write A and B in boxes and jot the one difference you clapped.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nA strong answer labels the boots line as A because it states one idea and repeats it, and labels the coats line as B because the words and rhythm are new. One real difference is enough: the second line does not echo \"boots in the hall\", and its rhythm is a short call plus two words rather than a doubled phrase. Saying \"the second line is B because it is second\" would also label an exact repeat as B, which is wrong. Quote a word from each section as evidence.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Any second line is section B, even when it copies the first line exactly in words and rhythm. That fails because an exact copy is more of section A, not a contrast. B is reserved for material that sounds different. If you cannot name a heard change, do not spend the letter B. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about structure?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nStructure is the order of sections, and this lesson uses two labels only. Section A is one musical idea. Section B is a different idea you can hear. If the first idea comes back unchanged, it is still A, because same material keeps the same letter. Different can mean the words, the rhythm, or both. A new line on the page is not automatically B. You will use a short original chant, spoken or softly sung, then label what you heard. Structure lets a listener predict a return or notice a real change. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 7: Aural skills and listening","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Aural skills and listening","week":5,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nChant A as \"Rain on the roof, rain on the roof\", using the same rhythm both times. Chant B as \"Thunder roll and then stop\", with a longer word on \"roll\" and a stop at the end. The form is A then B. B is not a repeat, because the words and the rhythm both change. Clap the rhythm of each line. The roof line splits into two matching halves. The thunder line does not. Write A and B in boxes and jot the one difference you clapped.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Two boxes contrast a repeated section A with a different section B.","caption":"B is a heard contrast, not merely the second line.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Section A</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Same chant heard again</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Section B</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">A chant that sounds different</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Two boxes contrast a same section with a different section.","caption":"An exact repeat keeps the letter A.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Same</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Words and rhythm return</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Different</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Words or rhythm change</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s5-music-y9-w04-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
